  grid: 19x19 [observed]
  colors:
    separator: 1 color; forms a solid cross (full row + full column, same color)
    quadrant_dominant: 4 distinct colors, one per quadrant; all 5 key colors mutually distinct
    noise: remaining colors (0-9 minus the 5 key colors); 6-8 distinct noise colors per quadrant
  cross:
    exactly 1 solid row and 1 solid col sharing the same color
    position: row 6-11, col 6-10; each quadrant has min dimension >=6 [observed]
  quadrants:
    interior (excluding 1-pixel border ring of the quadrant): 100% filled with the dominant color
    border ring: ~70-100% noise pixels; noise drawn randomly from all non-dominant colors (includes other dom colors, sep, and non-key colors)
    dominant color is the unique statistical mode (50-73% of total quadrant area)
  output: 3x3; corners = quadrant dominant colors, middle row/col = separator color

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick 5 distinct colors for the 3x3 output (sep + 4 quadrant dominants).
    2. Build the 3x3 output grid (corners = dominants, cross = sep).
    3. Choose grid size 19x19 and cross position (row, col) ensuring all quadrants have min dimension >=6.
    4. Fill cross row and cross column entirely with sep color.
    5. For each quadrant, fill the interior rectangle (all but the 1-pixel border ring) with its dominant color.
    6. Fill each border pixel of each quadrant with a random non-dominant color (any of the other 9 colors), ensuring the dominant remains the unique mode (>50% of quadrant).
